[Music]  time now for our morning rounds medical  news of the week when it comes to  colorectal cancer the latest data shows  an overall decline in cases for older  Americans but for those 55 and younger  it's a different story earlier this week  the American Cancer Society published  updated colorectal cancer screening  guidelines for those at average risk in  a major change they now say screenings  should start earlier than previously  suggested here to discuss this and more  is CBS News medical contributor dr.  David agus who joins us bright and early  from Los Angeles David good morning good  morning Anthony so what are the  headlines coming out of this new study  it's pretty bright first of all calling  people over the 50th age of 55 or older  is kind of scary  older Americans but we're seeing this  trend of colon cancer happening in  earlier earlier in younger people you  know I had a case in their 20s of colon  cancer several decades ago that was  unheard of but for some reason there's a  demographic shift and so now their new  recommendations are to get screaming  which is predominantly colonoscopy the  gold standard would happen at age 45  instead of 50 that's a major shift in  this country remember there's still  50,000 people a year dying of colon  cancer which is predominantly  preventable if you have a screening test  you said there's a demographic shift  some reason do we know potentially why  we're seeing this in younger people I  certainly wish I mean it whether it's  that we're larger we're more sedentary  our diets have changed we don't know the  exact reason but we're seeing this shift  and it's rather surprising this increase  and you know a reaction to the increase  is the hopefully that we can reduce  these deaths over 3,000 deaths a year  and people under the age of 50 from  colon cancer so the shift hopefully will  reduce the numbers of death in that  demographic David you mentioned  colonoscopy which is kind of as the gold  standard of testing but there are other  types of testing I mean how do you  decide what to do here there are other  types of testing you know colonoscopy  every 10 years there's sigmoidoscopy  there's the tests where they look for  blood in your stool have it  discussion with your doctor of what's  the right one each of them have risks  and benefits and none of them are  perfect but certainly have one of them  and have the right one based on your  family history and your risk profile to  hopefully prevent this disease alright  our next topic deals with terminally ill  patients and experimental drugs on  Wednesday President Trump signed into  law the right to try act it allows  certain drugs not yet approved by the  Food and Drug Administration to be  administered determinately ill patients  who have run out of other options David  break this legislation down for us a  little further if you could so this is  something that President Trump announced  in his State of the Union address in  January that is that drugs that are not  yet on the market they finished initial  testing what we call phase 1 testing and  at people with end-stage illness so you  know don't have any other standard  treatments to get can get these drugs  right now I give them to patients  routinely with the FDA's permission the  FDA has approved these drugs 99 plus  percent of the time and it's usually  within 24 to 48 hours we can give these  drugs what this new legislation says is  is that we're gonna take the FDA out of  the picture that the individual can  negotiate the doctor and the patient  with the pharmaceutical company and get  the drug without the FDA's involvement  just because people can get the drug  though David it doesn't mean the  pharmaceutical companies necessarily  will be giving them the drug so what are  the obstacles here no question about it  is that I don't think this will change  very much what's going on we routinely  can get drugs we call it compassionate  use in patients who have no other  treatments and pharmaceutical companies  many times do this but this legislation  doesn't mandate the pharmaceutical may  do it and there are a lot of risks for  the pharmaceutical company there could  be side effects the patient can  potentially even sue the pharmaceutical  company and they could take the focus  off of developing the drug and maybe  slow it for other patients and it's  actually costly and so I'm not sure this  legislation is going to do a lot there's  certainly a lot of press or media about  it a lot of hype around it but doesn't  really change the landscape in treating  patients with end-stage disease alright  one last topic David as the weather gets  warmer so does the urge to spend time  side but are you paying enough attention  to protecting your skin prolonged an  unprotected exposure to the Sun could  lead to melanoma the most dangerous form  of skin cancer according to the American  Cancer Society more than 91 thousand new  cases of melanoma will be diagnosed this  year alone a majority will involve men  accounting for a little over fifty five  thousand of these cases so some basic  ideas that people should keep in mind to  just prevent any damage from the Sun not  just melanoma so UVA UVB lights from the  Sun can damage skin wear sunscreen  especially between the hours of 10:00  and 4:00 a cloudy day UV light goes  right through the clouds so it doesn't  matter whether it's cloudy or it's sunny  wear sunscreen and protection the best  thing above sunscreen is wear long  sleeve cotton shirts wear a hat try to  keep your skin out of that Sun you will  thank yourself years two decades down  the road because you'll prevent skin  cancer dr. David agus thanks so much for  being with us this morning

